# Smart Node -- declared permission surface.
#
# THE list. This file is the only place where "who may write here" is decided
# for a Smart Node. Companion installers create directories; they do not choose
# their permissions. permissions.sh reads this file, applies it, and refuses on
# anything writable that is not declared here.
#
# A node adds its own state paths in /etc/smart-node/permissions.local.list
# (same format). Those entries may only lie under /var/lib/smart-node or /srv
# and may only add: a path declared here cannot be redeclared there.
#
# Format, whitespace-separated:
#
#     PATH   OWNER   GROUP   MODE   [FLAG]   [# note]
#
#   FLAG   cover  -- the subtree is exempt from the undeclared-writable scan
#          tree   -- ENFORCED: nothing under this path may be writable beyond
#                    its owner. Strips group/other write recursively; does NOT
#                    chown recursively (a component tree legitimately mixes
#                    ownership, and rewriting that breaks components for
#                    reasons unrelated to this boundary).
#
#   PATH   absolute; a trailing /* matches one level (e.g. /srv/agents/*)
#   OWNER  user name, numeric uid (for in-container uids), or "-" to leave the
#          owner untouched (per-agent dirs, where the owner differs per agent)
#   GROUP  group name, numeric gid, or "-" to leave the group untouched
#   MODE   4 octal digits, setgid/sticky included (e.g. 2755, 3775, 0640)
#
# A path that does not exist is SKIPPED, not failed: the component that owns it
# is simply not installed on this node.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The rule this file exists to hold
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Two groups, and the asymmetry between them is the whole boundary:
#
#   smart-node          -- SERVICE group. Component service accounts. They
#                          legitimately share core state with each other.
#   smart-node-agents   -- AGENT group. agent-<slug> accounts run here.
#
#   A service user MAY join smart-node-agents where it genuinely shares state
#   with agents (core-api provisions their credential; service crons write to
#   the same log tree). That grants agents nothing.
#
#   An agent user may NEVER join smart-node. That is the boundary. Every
#   group-write below is safe only because of it.
#
# History, so the next reader does not re-derive it: until 2026-07-10 the group
# `smart-node` contained service accounts only, and every group-write in this
# tree was written under that assumption. agent users were given that group as
# their primary, to fix a real bug (an agent's wake-cron could not write its own
# log dir). The directory modes were never revisited. This file, and the split
# above, are the correction. See the matching group and ACL rules in bootstrap/groups.sh and bootstrap/acls.sh.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- core tree -------------------------------------------------------------
# setgid is KEPT (per-component subdirs inherit the group); only group-write
# goes. Components own and write their own subtrees.
/opt/smart-node                        root                 smart-node         2755
# `tree`: the whole component tree was group-writable, so an agent could not
# merely add a file beside the mail adapter -- it could APPEND TO adapter.py,
# which a different service user then executes. Demonstrated on a live node
# and reverted.
/opt/smart-node/communication          smart-node-communication smart-node     2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/communication/state    smart-node-communication smart-node     0750

# Every other component tree, same rule: nothing under it writable beyond its
# owner. Owner and group are left alone (`-`) because each component owns its
# own tree and that ownership is the component's business, not this list's.
#
# On a node installed from canon these are already compliant and the entries do
# nothing. They exist for nodes where a component was cloned or edited in place:
# such trees are live git working copies, group-writable throughout, so every
# agent can rewrite the dispatcher, core-api and admin-runner code that other
# services execute. A path that does not exist is skipped, so listing components
# a given node does not have is free.
/opt/smart-node/core-api               -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/dispatcher             -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/admin-runner           -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/gateway                -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/studio                 -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/hitl                   root                 smart-node         2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/flows                  -                    -                  2755  tree
# The node's own flows, published by core-api, and the Runner's run directories
# for them. Owner-written only: core-api owns flows/, the dispatcher its run
# directories; the tree flag keeps anyone else from gaining write here.
/var/lib/smart-node/workflows          -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/mail                   -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/rein                   -                    -                  2755  tree
/opt/smart-node/workflows              -                    -                  2755  tree
# NOT listed with `tree`: memory, tracking and documents. Those are data trees,
# not code trees, and at least one of them (documents) legitimately holds a
# group-writable subtree -- Doci's own file store, declared separately below.
# A recursive write-strip there would break the component it is meant to guard.

# --- the projection tree ---------------------------------------------------
# core-api renders agent dirs, the tools registry and generated files here.
# It is the writer; nobody else needs to be.
# `tree` on all three: core-api is the only writer. A node with a hand-built
# tools tree (connectors, host-utils, planner) can carry it group-writable
# throughout -- meaning any agent could rewrite the very tools it is trusted to
# run. Per-agent runtime dirs below keep their own entries; tree only strips
# write beyond the owner, and 2750 has none to strip.
/srv/agents                            smart-node-core-api  smart-node         2755  tree
/srv/agents/_specialists               smart-node-core-api  smart-node         2755
/srv/tools                             smart-node-core-api  smart-node         2755  tree
/srv/generated                         smart-node-core-api  smart-node         2755  tree

# --- identity provider -----------------------------------------------------
# The authum container writes credentials.json and the rate-limit files as
# in-container uid 82. Own the dirs by that uid instead of opening them to the
# The agent runtime user's home, which happens to hold the LLM CLI that every
# agent is spawned to run (/usr/local/bin/claude symlinks into it). 0751, not
# 0750, for the same reason as /var/log/smart-node below: at 0750 an agent user
# cannot TRAVERSE to the binary, so the gateway starts a session, returns 200,
# and the agent answers with an empty string. A node from a clean install was
# born unable to think and nothing errored --
# Traverse only: --x for other, so nobody outside the group can list the home.
# The fix is a declared mode, NOT group membership: an agent user may never
# join the service group, which is the whole boundary.
/var/lib/smart-node/agent              smart-node-agent     smart-node         0751
/var/lib/smart-node/hitl               smart-node-hitl      smart-node         0700  tree
/var/lib/smart-node/hitl-queue         smart-node-hitl      smart-node-hitl-queue 2750 tree
/var/lib/smart-node/hitl-queue/delivered smart-node-hitl-delivery smart-node-hitl-queue 2710 tree
# world; core-api still writes users.json through the service group.
# Was 0777 on all three --
/var/lib/smart-node/authum             root                 smart-node         0750
/var/lib/smart-node/authum/auth        82                   smart-node         2770
/var/lib/smart-node/authum/data        82                   smart-node         2770
# Owner is the CONTAINER uid, not root: Authum's php-fpm workers run as 82 and
# READ this file on every password login. Tightening it to 0660 root:smart-node
# on the identity provider closed world-read -- and locked the identity provider out of its
# own account store, so password login returned a PHP warning instead of JSON.
# Passkey login kept working (credentials.json is already owned by 82), which is
# what hid it. Group stays smart-node: core-api writes it from /api/setup/complete.
/var/lib/smart-node/authum/auth/users.json 82                smart-node         0660
# Backups of the account store hold the same password hashes as the live file
# but nothing needs to read them. Root only.
/var/lib/smart-node/authum/auth/users.json.bak-* root        root               0600
/var/lib/smart-node/panel              82                   smart-node         2770

# --- secrets ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Group-readable/writable by the SERVICE group only: core-api reads the age key
# and writes the store. With agents out of that group this is closed.
/etc/smart-node/age-key.txt            root                 smart-node         0640
/etc/smart-node/integrations.sops.yaml root                 smart-node         0660

# --- agent-facing ----------------------------------------------------------
# What agents legitimately need. This is what must move WITH them, or they lose
# their model credential and stop working in a way that looks like an LLM outage.
/etc/smart-node/local                  root                 root               0700  tree  # node-local settings, loaded last by every smart-node unit; the release never writes inside (bootstrap/local-layer.sh)
/etc/smart-node/permissions.local.list root                root               0644        # the node's own state paths; read by permissions.sh, may only add under /var/lib/smart-node or /srv
/etc/smart-node/agent-runtime.env      root                 smart-node-agents  0640
/opt/smart-node/scratch                smart-node-agent     smart-node-agents  2770  cover
# The log root must be TRAVERSABLE by agents, or the cron log tree below is
# unreachable no matter what its own mode says. 0751: everyone can pass
# through, nobody but the owner can list it. Found by the positive half of the
# e2e test -- agents lost their cron log the moment they left the service
# group, which is exactly the "looks like an LLM outage" failure this task was
# warned about.
/var/log/smart-node                    smart-node           smart-node         0751
# Shared cron log tree: agents and service crons both append here. Sticky, so
# one job cannot delete another's log -- which before this it could.
/var/log/smart-node/cron               root                 smart-node-agents  3775
# Per-agent runtime dirs, written by the wake-cron running as the agent.
# 2750, not 0750: setgid is inherited from /srv/agents and re-applies itself
# immediately on this filesystem -- `chmod 0750` reads back as 2750. Harmless,
# because 2750 grants the group no write; declared as it really is so the
# stage converges instead of "fixing" the same four dirs forever.
/srv/agents/*/logs                     -                    smart-node-agents  2750
/srv/agents/*/state                    -                    smart-node-agents  2750
# The agent's own work area. Declared alongside logs/ and state/ for the
# same reason: admin-runner chowns it to the agent, and an undeclared dir the
# provisioner creates is what makes the next update refuse.
/srv/agents/*/work                     -                    smart-node-agents  2750

# --- project work areas --------------------------------------
# /srv/projects is the SHARED area: traversable by everyone so a member can
# reach their own project, writable by nobody, so a project cannot be created by
# making a directory. Only admin-runner's ensure_project creates them.
#
# `cover` because everything BENEATH is group-writable by design -- that is what
# a shared work area is -- and the undeclared-writable scan would otherwise
# refuse every project dir and fail every node update from the first project on.
# Found exactly that way: the scan flagged the tree the moment it existed.
#
# Covered is not unmanaged. The glob line below re-applies 2770 to each project
# dir on every run, so a directory that drifts to 0777 is corrected even though
# the scan no longer reports it.
/srv/projects                          root                 root               0755  cover
# Group is "-": it differs per project (project-<slug>), which is the whole
# access mechanism. setgid (the leading 2) is not decoration -- without it a
# file written by one member does not carry the group and the next member
# cannot read it, which fails on the second day rather than the first.
/srv/projects/*                        -                    -                  2770

# --- documents ---------------------------------------------------------------
# Doci's own file tree: group www-data, which no agent is in. Declared so the
# undeclared-writable scan does not flag it.
/opt/smart-node/documents/files        www-data             www-data           2775  cover
